Ladybird answers a redirected request with a 307 “Internal Redirect”
that carries the new Location, so the page’s next request goes on
under the same id. Chromium’s WebRequestProxyingURLLoaderFactory does
the same, adding a Non-Authoritative-Reason header. Firefox redirects
the channel itself instead (channel.redirectTo in WebRequest.sys.mjs),
which listeners also hear as a redirect under the same id. All
three allow a redirect to a data: URL or to the extension’s own
web-accessible resource, while Fetch still refuses a server’s redirect
to anything but HTTP(S).
